<Base title="Documentation — StreamSnipe"><div class="wrap"><article class="prose"><div class="eyebrow">The field guide / 01</div><h1>Get to know your studio.</h1><p class="lead">StreamSnipe consists of a local companion and a dashboard. Your companion does the recording; the dashboard is your control room.</p><h2>1. Run the companion</h2><p>Install and start the companion for your system when a release becomes available. The companion listens on this device at <code>127.0.0.1:47821</code>. Docker can also serve its own authenticated dashboard.</p><h2>2. Pair your browser</h2><p>Open the studio, choose Pair, and approve the request in the companion. Keep the companion running while using the dashboard. Chrome or Edge is recommended for the hosted dashboard; the bundled dashboard is available as a fallback.</p><h2>3. Add a channel</h2><p>Save a channel URL, choose whether to monitor and automatically record it, then check its current status. You can also start a recording directly from a URL.</p><h2>4. Explore your library</h2><p>Filter recordings, play completed media, or download a file. Settings control the recording folder, quality, retention, and optional direct uploads to your own S3-compatible storage.</p><h2>Troubleshooting</h2><p>If the studio cannot connect, check that the companion is running and that browser permission to access local network devices is allowed. If the protocol versions differ, update the companion. Pairings can be revoked from Settings.</p><p><a href="/app/">Open the studio →</a></p></article></div></Base>
